   ],
   "l": "The way of heaven —\nsurely it is like the stringing of a bow?\nWhat is high, press it down;\nwhat is low, raise it up;\nwhat has surplus, draw off from it;\nwhat falls short, make it up.\nThe way of heaven:\ndraws off from what has surplus and makes up what falls short.\nThe way of man is not so —",
   "v": true,
   "i": "The way of heaven — is it not like stringing a bow? The high end is pressed down, the low end lifted; where there is too much it is drawn off, where too little it is made up. That is heaven's way: it draws down what has too much and fills out what has too little. Man's way is not like that —",
   "n": "Chinese asks a question with particles, not with word order. 其 at the head of a clause is not 'its' here but a modal, softening the statement into a supposition, and 與 (read yú, not yǔ 'to give') closes it as a question: together, 'surely it is like…?' You met the same frame in chapter 5, 其猶橐籥乎, 'surely it is like a bellows?'\n\n張弓 is not shooting but stringing. To string a bow you set one limb against your foot, bend the high end down and lever the low end up until the loop catches; too much curve in one limb and you ease it, too little and you press. The image is the argument, and the four clauses that follow are simply what your hands do.\n\n高者抑之: 者 turns the bare adjective 高 into a noun, 'the high one', and then 之 picks that same thing up again as the object of the verb — a doubling English has no use for. Four times in four lines. 損 and 補 are a matched pair of workman's words: 損 to draw off, 補 (silk radical) to patch a hole and make the cloth whole.\n\nThe Mawangdui manuscripts are reported to read 益 yì, 'to increase', where the received summary line has 補 — the same sense, the plainer word.",
   "p": true
  },
  {
