   "l": "draws off from what falls short in order to serve what has surplus.\nWho is able to have surplus and thereby serve all under heaven?\nOnly the one who has the Way.\nTherefore the sage acts and yet does not presume on it,\nthe work completed, and he does not settle into it;\nhe has no wish to show worth.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "it takes from those who have too little in order to serve those who have too much. Who can have a surplus and put it at the service of all under heaven? Only the one who holds the Way. And so the sage acts and does not presume upon it; the work is finished and he does not settle into it, having no wish to display his worth.",
   "n": "奉 is a picture: two hands raised holding something up. It is the word for waiting on a superior and for carrying tribute, and it is exactly the wrong direction — man's way hauls what little the poor have uphill to the rich.\n\n孰 is 'who, which of them', and it stands at the head of its clause; the question mark is the modern editor's, and the only thing in the original that marks a question is 孰 itself.\n\n為而不恃 is one of the book's refrains: you have met it three times already, in chapters 2, 10 and 51. The line after it is a variation rather than a repeat — chapter 2 ends 功成而弗居 with 居 'dwell', where this chapter has 功成而不處 with 處, the same 不處 that chapters 24 and 31 used of the man who holds the Way. 恃 is to lean your weight on something, 處 is to move in and live there. Act, and do not lean on the act; finish, and do not take up residence in the finish.\n\n見賢 turns on the polyphone. Read jiàn the character is 'to see'; read xiàn it is the causative, 'to make seen, to put on show'. So this is not 'he does not wish to see worth' but 'he does not wish to display worth' — chapter 22 said the same of him — 不自見故明, 'he makes no show of himself, and so is seen clearly' — and chapter 72 repeats it as 自知不自見; chapter 24 puts it the other way round, 自見者不明.\n\n其 in that last clause can be taken as the plain pronoun 'he', or as the same modal particle that opened the bow question — 'he would not wish…'. Nothing decides it, and the sense does not move."
